186363091 1111 #uuid "f7ee10cd-fa47-5b46-ba62-d2f960e5ed4c" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363092 1034 "-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{3x}" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363092 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535719 true] [17592186363092 1111 #uuid "fcdfef37-bbf8-5505-9a2a-cfffb5967343" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363093 1034 "\\frac{5}{3}" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363093 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535719 true] [17592186363093 1111 #uuid "a9635bcb-87ba-5bd9-821a-7bc306af97dd" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363094 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363094 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535719 true] [17592186363094 1111 #uuid "da24722d-c932-5e0a-aa9b-4a0d98d8c45c" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363095 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363096 13194139535719 true] [17592186363095 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363102 13194139535719 true] [17592186363095 1017 #edb/ref 17592186363107 13194139535719 true] [17592186363095 1089 "q-337399" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363095 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535719 true] [17592186363095 1100 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." 13194139535719 true] [17592186363095 1113 #uuid "a129942b-bc96-5e41-9abc-674cc7d1e75b" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363095 1145 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363097 13194139535719 true] [17592186363096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363098 13194139535719 true] [17592186363096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363099 13194139535719 true] [17592186363096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363100 13194139535719 true] [17592186363096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363101 13194139535719 true] [17592186363096 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535719 true] [17592186363096 1041 "field-1" 13194139535719 true] [175