mplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =10{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x=\\frac{{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =10{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\  & =10{e}^{-5x}(\\frac{{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}}{2}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-5x}({e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =5+5{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =5+5{e}^{-10x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5+5{e}^{-10x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =5x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =5x{e}^{5x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363114 13194139535719 true] [17592186363113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363115 13194139535719 true] [17592186363113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363116 13194139535719 true] [17592186363113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363117 13194139535719 true] [17592186363113 1000 #edb/ref 17592186363118 13194139535719 true] [17592186363113 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535719 true] [17592186363113 1041 "field-1" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363113 1104 #edb/ref 17592186363116 13194139535719 true] [17592186363113 1110 #uuid "f30a8b5c-cda7-5b6a-ac19-afc6dcbfa075" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363114 1034 "u(x){e}^{10x}" 13194139535719 true] [17592186363114 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535719